round, and starts heading back to Q. Then at some point, they meet again. The question is, when is that second meeting?\n\nI need to model their movements over time. Maybe I can track their positions as functions of time. Let\u2019s set t = 0 as the start time. Let\u2019s define the position along the road from P to Q as a coordinate from 0 to D. So Alex starts at 0 (P) moving towards D (Q), and Bob starts at D (Q) moving towards 0 (P).\n\nLet\u2019s first find where they meet the first time. At time t = 15 minutes, Alex has traveled 3v * 15 = 45v. Bob has traveled v * 15 = 15v. So Alex is at position 45v from P, Bob is at position D - 15v from P. But they meet at the same point, so 45v = D - 15v. Therefore, 45v + 15v = D => 60v = D. Which matches what we had before. So D = 60v. So their first meeting is at position 45v from P, which is 45v / 60v = 3/4 of the way from P to Q.\n\nNow, after meeting, both continue to their destinations. Let's find out how long it takes each to reach their destinations.\n\nFor Alex: After meeting at 45v from P, he needs to go the remaining 15v to reach Q. His speed is 3v, so time = distance/speed = 15v / 3v = 5 minutes. So he arrives at Q at t = 15 + 5 = 20 minutes. Then he immediately turns around and starts heading back to P at speed 3v.\n\nFor Bob: After meeting at 45v from P (which is D - 15v = 60v -15v=45v from Q), he needs to go the remaining 15v to reach P. His speed is v, so time = 15v / v = 15 minutes. So he arrives at P at t = 15 + 15 = 30 minutes. Then he immediately turns around and starts heading back to Q at speed v.\n\nSo between t=15 and t=20, Alex is moving towards Q, Bob is moving towards P. From t=20 to t=30, Alex is moving back towards P, while Bob is still moving towards P until t=30. Then after t=30, both are moving towards each other again: Alex is going from Q to P (since he turned around at t=20), but wait, hold on. Wait, Alex turned around at t=20 to go back to P. Bob is still going to P until t=30, then turns around to go back to Q.\n\nWait, this seems a bit complicated. Maybe we need to model their positions as functions of time.\n\nLet me define the position functions:\n\nFor Alex:\n- From t=0 to t=20: moving from P to Q. Position at time t: 3v * t. (since speed is 3v)\n- At t=20, he reaches Q (position D = 60v). Then from t=20 onwards, he moves back towards P at speed 3v. So his position is D - 3v*(t - 20) = 60v - 3v*(t - 20).\n\nFor Bob:\n- From t=0 to t=30: moving from Q to P. Position at time t: D - v * t = 60v - v*t.\n- At t=30, he reaches P (position 0). Then from t=30 onwards, he moves back towards Q at speed v. So his position is v*(t - 30).\n\nNow, we need to find the second meeting time. The first meeting is at t=15. The next meeting will be after that. Let's check between t=20 and t=30, and after t=30.\n\nFirst, let's check between t=20 and t=30.\n\nBetween t=20 and t=30:\n- Alex is moving from Q to P at speed 3v. His position: 60v - 3v*(t - 20) = 60v -3vt +60v = 120v -3vt. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, position when moving back is D - speed*(time after turnaround). So at t=20, position is 60v. Then each minute after that, he moves back 3v. So position at time t >20: 60v - 3v*(t -20).\n\nSimilarly, Bob is moving from Q to P until t=30. So his position until t=30 is 60v -v*t.\n\nWait, but at t=20, Bob's position is 60v -20v=40v. So at t=20, Alex is at 60v (Q), Bob is at 40v. Then from t=20 to t=30, Alex is moving left (towards P) at 3v, Bob is moving left (towards P) at v. So their positions as functions of t (for 20 \u2264 t \u226430):\n\nAlex: 60v -3v(t -20)\nBob: 60v -v*t\n\nWe need to find if they meet between t=20 and t=30. So set their positions equal:\n\n60v -3v(t -20) = 60v -v*t\n\nSimplify:\n\n60v -3v(t -20) = 60v -v*t\n\nSubtract 60v from both sides:\n\n-3v(t -20) = -v*t\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n3v(t -20) = v*t\n\nDivide both sides by v (assuming v \u22600):\n\n3(t -20) = t\n\n3t -60 = t\n\n3t - t =60\n\n2t =60\n\nt=30\n\nSo they meet at