x took 5 minutes to reach Q, arriving at 20 minutes. Then Bob was supposed to take 15 minutes to reach P, arriving at 30 minutes. Wait, this is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe the mistake comes from the initial assumption. Let's recap.\n\nWe said that at the first meeting, which is at t=15, Alex has traveled 3v*15=45v. Bob has traveled v*15=15v. Since they met, the sum of their distances should be D. So 45v +15v=60v=D. Therefore, D=60v.\n\nTherefore, after meeting at t=15:\n\n- Alex is 45v away from P, so he has D -45v=15v left to Q. Since his speed is 3v, time to Q is 15v/(3v)=5 minutes. So arrives at Q at t=15+5=20.\n\n- Bob is 15v away from Q (since he started at Q and traveled 15v towards P), so he has D -15v=45v left to P. His speed is v, so time to P is 45v /v=45 minutes. So arrives at P at t=15+45=60.\n\nWait, that contradicts my previous conclusion where I thought Bob arrives at P at t=30. Where did that come from? Probably a mistake in the earlier analysis.\n\nSo if Bob has 45v left to P at t=15, then he takes 45 minutes to reach P, arriving at t=60. Therefore, Bob doesn't turn around until t=60. That's different. So prior error was assuming Bob arrives at P at 30 minutes, but that's incorrect. So Bob actually arrives at P at t=60, turns around, and goes back to Q.\n\nSimilarly, Alex arrives at Q at t=20, turns around, and goes back to P. So between t=20 and t=60, Alex is going from Q to P at 3v speed. Bob is still going from Q to P until t=60.\n\nWait, this changes things. So from t=15 to t=20, Alex is moving to Q, Bob is moving to P.\n\nAt t=20, Alex is at Q, turns around. From t=20 to t=60, Alex is moving from Q to P at 3v. Bob is moving from Q to P at v until t=60, then turns around.\n\nSo during t=20 to t=60, Alex is moving towards P at 3v, and Bob is moving towards P at v. So their positions:\n\nAlex's position from t=20 to t=60: starting at Q (60v), moving back to P at 3v. So position at time t is 60v -3v*(t -20).\n\nBob's position from t=15 to t=60: starting at 45v from Q (which is 15v from P) at t=15, moving towards P at v. So his position as a function of time: At t=15, he's at 15v from P. Then, from t=15 to t=60, he travels towards P. So position is 15v + v*(t -15). Wait, hold on. Wait, position from P is 15v at t=15, and he is moving towards P at speed v. Wait, moving towards P would decrease the distance from P, but adding v*(t-15) would increase it. Wait, that's not right.\n\nWait, actually, if we set up the coordinate system with P at 0 and Q at D=60v, then Bob's position when moving from Q to P is D - v*t. Wait, but at t=0, he is at D=60v. At t=15, he is at 60v -15v=45v from Q, which is 15v from P. Wait, maybe better to model positions from P.\n\nLet me re-define positions as distance from P. So:\n\n- Alex starts at 0 (P) and moves towards Q (60v) at 3v per minute.\n\n- Bob starts at 60v (Q) and moves towards P at v per minute.\n\nFirst meeting occurs at t=15. At that time:\n\nAlex has moved 3v*15=45v, so his position is 45v from P.\n\nBob has moved v*15=15v towards P, so his position is 60v -15v=45v from P. So they meet at 45v.\n\nAfter that:\n\nAlex continues towards Q. From t=15 to t=20, he covers the remaining 15v to Q at 3v per minute (15v /3v=5 minutes). So arrives at Q (60v) at t=20, then turns around and heads back to P at 3v per minute.\n\nBob continues towards P. From t=15 onward, he has 45v left to P (since he was at 45v at t=15). At v per minute, it takes him 45 minutes, so he arrives at P (0v) at t=15+45=60. Then he turns around and heads back to Q at v per minute.\n\nSo between t=20 and t=60, Alex is moving from Q to P at 3v, and Bob is moving from Q to P at v until t=60, then he turns around.\n\nWait, but from t=20 to t=60, Alex is moving towards P, and Bob is also moving towards P but slower. So Alex is catching up to Bob? Wait, no. If both are moving in the same direction, towards P, then their relative speed is 3v - v = 2v. So Alex is gaining on Bob at 2v per minute. But they are both mov