e we don't need to go all the way to 99. Let's see.\n\nBut first, let's check if my categorization is correct. For example, when does the square of a number become three digits? The smallest number whose square is three digits is 10 (10\u00b2=100), and the largest number with a three-digit square is 31 (31\u00b2=961). Then 32\u00b2=1024 is four digits. So that's correct. So numbers 10-31 (inclusive) have 3-digit squares. Then numbers 32-99 have 4-digit squares.\n\nNow, let's calculate cumulative digits up to each category to see where the 100th digit falls.\n\nFirst category: numbers 1-3 (1-digit squares): 3 digits. So positions 1-3.\n\nSecond category: numbers 4-9 (2-digit squares): each contributes 2 digits. 6 numbers, so 12 digits. So cumulative digits after numbers 1-9: 3 +12=15 digits. Positions 4-15.\n\nThird category: numbers 10-31 (3-digit squares): 22 numbers, each 3 digits. So 22*3=66 digits. Cumulative digits after numbers 1-31: 15 +66=81 digits. Positions 16-81.\n\nFourth category: numbers 32-99 (4-digit squares): 68 numbers, each 4 digits. 68*4=272 digits. So cumulative digits after 1-99: 81 +272=353 digits. But we need the 100th digit, which is beyond 81 digits (which ends at position 81). Therefore, the 100th digit must be within the fourth category, which is the 4-digit squares starting from number 32.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, cumulative digits up to 31: 81. Then the next digits come from numbers starting at 32. Each of these numbers contributes 4 digits. So starting from position 82, the digits from 32\u00b2=1024 would be 1,0,2,4 (positions 82,83,84,85). Then 33\u00b2=1089: positions 86,87,88,89. And so on.\n\nWe need to find the 100th position. So how many digits do we need after position 81? 100 -81=19 digits into the 4-digit numbers. So we need to find which 4-digit square's digits contain the 19th digit in this sequence.\n\nSince each 4-digit square contributes 4 digits, we can divide 19 by 4 to find how many numbers we need to go through and