 is the fifth number. So yes, after 4 numbers, we reach position 97, then 36 starts at 98.\n\nAnother way: cumulative digits from 32 onwards:\n\nNumber of 4-digit numbers needed to reach the 100th position:\n\nWe need 100 -81 =19 digits from 4-digit numbers.\n\nEach 4-digit number gives 4 digits. So 19 /4 =4.75. So 4 full numbers (32-35) give 16 digits, reaching up to position 81+16=97. Then, the next number (36) gives the next 4 digits starting at 98. Therefore, the 19th digit is the 3rd digit of 36\u00b2, which is 9.\n\nYes, this seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, let's list the positions step by step:\n\nPositions 1-3: 1,4,9 (from 1\u00b2,2\u00b2,3\u00b2)\n\nPositions 4-15: 16 (4\u00b2),25 (5\u00b2),36 (6\u00b2),49 (7\u00b2),64 (8\u00b2),81 (9\u00b2). Each of these is two digits, so 6 numbers *2 digits=12 digits. So positions 4-15.\n\nWait, let's break that down:\n\n4\u00b2=16: positions 4 (1),5 (6)\n\n5\u00b2=25: positions 6 (2),7 (5)\n\n6\u00b2=36: positions8 (3),9 (6)\n\n7\u00b2=49: positions10 (4),11 (9)\n\n8\u00b2=64: positions12 (6),13 (4)\n\n9\u00b2=81: positions14 (8),15 (1)\n\nSo positions 4-15 are filled as such. Then numbers 10-31 (3-digit squares):\n\nEach contributes 3 digits. 22 numbers, 22*3=66 digits. So positions16-81.\n\nLet me check with a few examples:\n\n10\u00b2=100: positions16 (1),17 (0),18 (0)\n\n11\u00b2=121: positions19 (1),20 (2),21 (1)\n\n12\u00b2=144: positions22 (1),23 (4),24 (4)\n\n...\n\n31\u00b2=961: positions81 (1) [Wait, let's check: cumulative digits up to 9:15. Then 22 numbers with 3 digits each: 66 digits. 15+66=81. So the last digit of 31\u00b2 is at position81. Let's verify 31\u00b2=961: digits 9,6,1. So the three digits would be positions79,80,81. Therefore, position81 is the last digit of 31\u00b2, which is 1.\n\nThen, starting from 32\u00b2=1024:\n\n32\u00b2=1024: digits1,0,2,4 \u2192 positions82,83,84,85\n\n33\u00b2=1089: digits1,0,8,9 \u2192 positions86,87,88,89\n\n34\u00b2=1156: digits1,1,5,6 \u2192 positions90,91,9