n\n\u222b 1 dt = t + C\n\n\u222b 1/(7 + t\u00b2) dt = (1/\u221a7) arctan(t/\u221a7) + C\n\nTherefore, I\u2081 = t - 6*(1/\u221a7) arctan(t/\u221a7) + C\n\nNow, revert back to x. Since t = tan x, so:\n\nI\u2081 = tan x - (6/\u221a7) arctan(tan x / \u221a7) + C\n\nTherefore, evaluating from 0 to \u03c0/3:\n\nI\u2081 = [ tan(\u03c0/3) - (6/\u221a7) arctan(tan(\u03c0/3)/\u221a7) ] - [ tan 0 - (6/\u221a7) arctan(0) ]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\ntan(\u03c0/3) = \u221a3, tan 0 = 0, arctan(0) = 0.\n\nThus:\n\nI\u2081 = \u221a3 - (6/\u221a7) arctan(\u221a3 / \u221a7) - 0 + 0 = \u221a3 - (6/\u221a7) arctan(\u221a(3/7))\n\nSo now, going back to the original integral I = I\u2081 - I\u2082:\n\nI = [\u221a3 - (6/\u221a7) arctan(\u221a(3/7))] - [ (1/\u221a7) arctan(\u221a(3/7)) ] = \u221a3 - [ (6/\u221a7 + 1/\u221a7) arctan(\u221a(3/7)) ]\n\nCombine the terms in the brackets:\n\n6/\u221a7 + 1/\u221a7 = 7/\u221a7 = \u221a7\n\nSo, I = \u221a3 - \u221a7 * arctan(\u221a(3/7))\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check that arithmetic again. 6/\u221a7 + 1/\u221a7 = (6 + 1)/\u221a7 = 7/\u221a7. 7 divided by \u221a7 is \u221a7 because \u221a7*\u221a7=7. So 7/\u221a7 = \u221a7. Therefore, yes, the coefficient becomes \u221a7.\n\nTherefore, the integral simplifies to:\n\nI = \u221a3 - \u221a7 arctan(\u221a(3/7))\n\nBut let me check if this is the simplest form. Maybe we can rationalize the argument inside arctan. Since \u221a(3/7) is the same as (\u221a21)/7. Alternatively, perhaps express it as arctan(\u221a3 / \u221a7). Either way is acceptable. But maybe we can further simplify arctan(\u221a(3/7)) using some trigonometric identities or relate it to another angle? Let's see.\n\nSuppose \u03b8 = arctan(\u221a(3/7)), then tan \u03b8 = \u221a(3/7). Let me consider a right triangle where the opposite side is \u221a3 and the adjacent side is \u221a7. Then the hypotenuse would be \u221a( (\u221a3)^2 + (\u221a7)^2 ) = \u221a(3 + 7) = \u221a10. Therefore, \u03b8 is an angle in such a triangle. Not sure if tha