 -x^3 + 6x^2 - 12x + 8\n\nCompute -3(-x + 2)^2:\n\n= -3(x^2 - 4x + 4) = -3x^2 + 12x - 12\n\nCompute 3(-x + 2):\n\n= -3x + 6\n\nAdd them up:\n\n(-x^3 + 6x^2 - 12x + 8) + (-3x^2 + 12x - 12) + (-3x + 6)\n\n= -x^3 + (6x^2 - 3x^2) + (-12x + 12x - 3x) + (8 - 12 + 6)\n\n= -x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 2\n\nNow compute g(f(x)) = -f(x) + 2 = -x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 2\n\nThey are equal! So f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x satisfies the equation for a = -1 and b = 2. Therefore, higher-degree polynomials are possible when a = -1.\n\nThis suggests that when a = -1, there exist solutions of any odd degree, provided the coefficients satisfy certain conditions. Similarly, for a = -1 and even degrees, it's impossible because of the leading term condition. Wait, but in this example, the cubic polynomial worked even though a = -1. Therefore, my previous conclusion that when a = -1, polynomials of odd degree can exist is correct. So for a = -1, there are solutions of any odd degree, and possibly other solutions as well, but with the coefficients satisfying certain relations.\n\nBut how to characterize all such polynomials?\n\nThis seems complicated. However, perhaps there's a general form for polynomials f that commute with g(x) = a x + b under composition. For linear functions, we already have a characterization. For higher-degree polynomials, it depends on the value of a.\n\nFrom the leading term analysis, unless a^n = a for the degree n of f, there are no solutions. Therefore, for a given a, the possible degrees n must satisfy a^n = a. For real a:\n\n- If a = 0: Then 0^n = 0, which holds for any n \u2265 1. However, when a = 0, g(x) = b, so f(g(x)) = f(b) must equal g(f(x)) = a f(x) + b = 0 + b = b. Therefore, f(b) = b, regardless of the degree of f. Therefore, for a = 0, any polynomial f (of any degree) that satisfies f(b) = b works.\n\n- If a = 1: Then 1^n = 1 holds for any n. So polynomials of any degree may potentially work. But as seen earlier, when a = 1 and b \u2260 0, only linear polynomials of the f