mod 2. Correct.\n\nEquation 3: x2 + x3 + x4 = 1 + 1 + 1 = 1. Correct.\n\nEquation 4: x3 + x4 + x0 = 1 + 1 + 1 = 1. Correct.\n\nSo the solution is all x_i =1. Pressing each button once. Let's check if this works. Each light is toggled three times (by its own button and the two neighbors). Starting from all on, three toggles would turn each off (since 3 is odd). So yes, pressing all buttons once works for n=5.\n\nWait, so for n=3,4,5 pressing all buttons once works. Maybe for odd n, pressing all buttons once works? But n=4 is even and pressing all once works. Wait, in n=4, pressing all buttons once toggles each light 3 times, which is equivalent to 1 modulo 2. So starting from all on, pressing all once turns them all off. Similarly for n=5, pressing all once toggles each 3 times, turning them off.\n\nWait, but in general, pressing each button once will toggle each light three times, regardless of n. So for any n, pressing all buttons once will toggle each light three times. Since 3 mod 2 is 1, this is equivalent to toggling each light once, which would turn all lights off if they started on. Therefore, regardless of n, pressing every button once should solve the problem.\n\nBut wait, that can't be right. For example, if n=1, pressing the single button toggles itself and its two neighbors, but n=1, so neighbors are itself. So pressing it once toggles it three times, which is equivalent to once. So turns it off. Yes. For n=2, pressing each button once. Each light is button 0 and 1. Each press toggles three buttons, but in a circle of two, the neighbors of 0 are 1 and 1 (since 0-1= -1 \u22611 mod 2, and 0+1=1). Wait, n=2:\n\nEach button press toggles the pressed button and its two neighbors. For n=2, each button press toggles three buttons: the pressed one and the two adjacent, but in a circle of two, each button has two neighbors, which are the same as the other button. So pressing button 0 toggles button 0, button 1, and button 1 again? Wait, no. Wait, in a circle of two, each button has two distinct neighbors. For example, button 0's neighbors are button 1 and button 1? No, that can't be. Wait, in a circle of two, each button has two neighbors: for button 0, neighbors are 1 and 1? Wait, no, in a circle of two buttons, each has two neighbors: button 0's neighbors are button 1 and button 1? That can't be. Wait, maybe in a circle of two, each button has two different neighbors? Wait, no, in a circle of two, each button is adjacent to the other button on both sides. So pressing button 0 would toggle button 0, button 1, and button 1 again. Wait, but that's toggling button 1 twice, which cancels out. So pressing button 0 toggles button 0 once and button 1 twice. So effectively, pressing button 0 toggles button 0 and button 1 once (since toggling twice is equivalent to not toggling). Wait, but that's not correct. If you press button 0, you toggle button 0, and its two neighbors (which are both button 1). So pressing button 0 would toggle button 0 once and button 1 twice. Toggling twice is the same as not toggling, so effectively, pressing button 0 toggles button 0 only. Similarly, pressing button 1 toggles button 1 only. Therefore, for n=2, pressing each button once would toggle each button once (from pressing itself) and twice from the neighbors (which cancel out). So pressing both buttons once would toggle each button once (since each is pressed once). Therefore, starting from all on, pressing both buttons once would toggle each once, turning them off. So even for n=2, pressing all buttons once works.\n\nWait, so maybe for any n, pressing all buttons once toggles each light three times. Wait, but in the case of n=2, pressing each button once would toggle each light three times. Let's see: button 0 is pressed once, toggling itself and neighbors (button 1 twice). Similarly, button 1 is pressed once, toggling itself and neighbors (button 0 twice). So each button is toggled 1 (from itself) + 2 (from neighbors) = 3 times. Which is equivalent to 1 mod 2. So yes, each light is toggled once, turning them off. So in general, pressing all buttons once works for any n? But that contradicts some intuition. For example, let's test n=1. Pressing the only button once toggles it three times (since its neighbors are itself). So three toggles, which is equivalent to one. Starting from on, turns it off. Works.\n\nBut wait, the original problem states that with one press of a button, the pressed button-light and its neighboring button-lights change their state. For n=1, pressing the button toggles it and its two neighbors, which are itself. So pressing it once toggles it three times. Similarly, for n=2, pressing a button toggles three buttons: itself and the two neighbors, which for n=2, the two neighbors are the same button. So pressing button 0 toggles button 0 and button 1 (twice). Wait, no. In n=2, button 0's neighbors are button 1 and button 1? No, neighbors are button 1 and button 1? That can't be. Wait, in a circle of two buttons arranged as 0 and 1. Each button has a left and right neighbor. For button 0: left neighbor is button 1, right neighbor is button 1. Similarly for button 1. So pressing button 0 toggles button 0, button 1, and button 1. So toggles button 0 once, button 1 twice. Similarly, pressing button 1 toggles button 1 once, button 0 twice. So pressing both buttons once: button 0 is toggled once (from pressing itself) + twice (from pressing button 1) = three times \u2192 equivalent to once. Button 1 is toggled once (from pressing itself) + twice (from pressing button 0) = three times \u2192 equivalent to once. So pressing both buttons once toggles each button once. So starting from all on, pressing both buttons once turns them all off. So yes, works for n=2.\n\nTherefore, it seems that for any n, pressing all buttons once will toggle each light three times, which is equivalent to toggling once, thus turning all lights off. Therefore, regardless of the number of buttons, this should work.\n\nBut