m**: The set of occupied chairs must be invariant under a rotation of \\(k\\) chairs. This implies the set is a union of orbits under the rotation by \\(k\\) chairs.\n2. **Orbit Analysis**: The length of each orbit (cycle) is \\(\\frac{20}{\\gcd(k, 20)}\\). The number of such orbits is \\(\\gcd(k, 20)\\).\n3. **Valid \\(n\\) Values**: For each \\(k\\), \\(n\\) must be a multiple of the orbit length \\(\\frac{20}{\\gcd(k, 20)}\\). The number of valid \\(n\\) values for each \\(k\\) is \\(\\gcd(k, 20)\\).\n4. **Counting Pairs**: For each divisor \\(d\\) of 20, the number of \\(k\\) values with \\(\\gcd(k, 20) = d\\) is \\(\\phi\\left(\\frac{20}{d}\\right)\\). The number of valid \\(n\\) values for each \\(d\\) is \\(d\\). Summing over all divisors \\(d\\) of 20 gives the total number of pairs.\n\nCalculations:\n- Divisors of 20: \\(1, 2, 4, 5, 10, 20\\).\n- For each divisor \\(d\\):\n  - Number of \\(k\\) values: \\(\\phi\\left(\\frac{20}{d}\\right)\\).\n  - Number of \\(n\\) values: \\(d\\).\n  - Contribution to total pairs: \\(\\phi\\left(\\frac{20}{d}\\right) \\times d\\).\n\nResults:\n- \\(d=1\\): \\(\\phi(20) = 8\\), contribution: \\(8 \\times 1 = 8\\).\n- \\(d=2\\): \\(\\phi(10) = 4\\), contribution: \\(4 \\times 2 = 8\\).\n- \\(d=4\\): \\(\\phi(5) = 4\\), contribution: \\(4 \\times 4 = 16\\).\n- \\(d=5\\): \\(\\phi(4) = 2\\), contribution: \\(2 \\times 5 = 10\\).\n- \\(d=10\\): \\(\\phi(2) = 1\\), contribution: \\(1 \\times 10 = 10\\).\n- \\(d=20\\): \\(\\phi(1) = 1\\), contribution: \\(1 \\times 20 = 20\\).\n\nSumming these contributions gives the total number of pairs:\n\\[8 + 8 + 16 + 10 + 10 + 20 = 72\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\(\\boxed{72}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to determine for how many pairs $(n, k)$ with $1 \\leq n \\leq 20$ and $1 \\leq k \\leq 20$ it is possible for people to move $k$ chairs and still have exactly the same set of chairs occupied.\n\n1. **Consider \\( k = 20 \\):**\n\n    - When \\( k = 20 \\), moving 20 chairs around the circle moves each person back to their original seat.\n    - Therefore, any configuration of people sitting in chairs is preserved by this movement.\n    - \\[\n    \\text{Thus, } n \\text{ can take any value from 1 to 20 when } k = 20.\n    \\]\n    - There are \\( 20 \\) pairs \\((n, k)\\) that work when \\( k = 20 \\).\n\n2. **Consider \\( k = 10 \\):**\n\n    - When \\( k = 10 \\), any people in chairs 1 to 10 move to chairs 11 to 20 and any people in chairs 11 to 20 move to chairs 1 to 10.\n    - This means:\n        - The two halves (1 to 10 and 11 to 20) must contain the same number of people in the same configuration.\n        - Hence, the total number of people in chairs is even.\n    - \\[\n    \\text{There are } 10 \\text{ possible values for \\( n \\) (even numbers from 2 to 20).}\n    \\]\n    - There are \\( 10 \\) pairs \\((n, k)\\) that work when \\( k = 10 \\).\n\n3. **Consider \\( k = 5 \\):**\n\n    - Here, any people in chairs:\n        - 1 to 5 move to chairs 6 to 10,\n        - 6 to 10 move to chairs 11 to 15,\n        - 11 to 15 move to chairs 16 to 20,\n        - 16 to 20 move to chairs 1 to 5.\n    - This means that the four sections containing 5 chairs must contain the same number of people in the same configuration.\n    - Hence, the total number of people in chairs is a multiple of 4, which can be \\( 4, 8, 12, 16, \\) or \\( 20 \\).\n    - \\[\n    \\text{Thus, there are } 5 \\text{ possible values of } n.\n    \\]\n    - There are \\( 5 \\) pairs \\((n, k)\\) that work when \\( k = 5 \\).\n\n4. **Consider \\( k = 4 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\):**\n\n    - Following the same logic:\n        - For \\( k = 4 \\): there are \\( 4 \\) possible values of \\( n \\) (multiples of 5): \\( 5, 10, 15, 20 \\).\n        - For \\( k = 2 \\): there are \\( 2 \\) possible values of \\( n \\) (multiples of 10): \\( 10, 20 \\).\n    - Thus, there are \\( 4 \\) pairs when \\( k = 4 \\) and \\( 2 \\) pairs when \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n5. **Consider \\( k = 1 \\):**\n\n    - Each person in a chair moves 1 chair along the circle.\n    - If there is a person in chair 1, they move to chair 2, implying chair 2 must have been occupied, and so on.\n    - \\[\n    \\text{Thus, all 20 chairs must be full, so } n = 20 \\text{ is the only possibility.}\n    \\]\n    - There is \\( 1 \\) pair when \\( k = 1 \\).\n\n6. **Consider \\( k = 3, k = 7, k = 9 \\):**\n\n    - Analogous to \\( k = 1 \\), a similar cycle argument can be constructed showing that all 20 chairs must be occupied.\n    - \\[\n    \\text{Thus, for each of } k = 3, k = 7, \\text{ and } k = 9, n = 20 \\text{ is the only possibility.}\n    \\]\n    - There are \\( 1 \\) pair each for \\( k = 3, k = 7, k = 9 \\).\n\n7. **Consider \\( k = 6 \\):**\n\n    - Looking at the cycles:\n    - Two cycles are generated covering all 20 chairs, so set of 10 chairs are either all occupied or not.\n    - \\[\n    \\text{Possible values of } n \\text{ are } 10 \\text{ or } 20.\n    \\]\n    - There are \\( 2 \\) pairs when \\( k = 6 \\).\n\n8. **Consider \\( k = 8 \\):**\n\n    - Generates four cycles covering all 20 chairs.\n    - \\[\n    \\text{Possible values of } n \\text{ are } 5, 10, 15, 20.\n    \\]\n    - There are \\( 4 \\) pairs when \\( k = 8 \\).\n\n9. **Consider \\( k = 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19 \\):**\n\n    - These are the same as corresponding values of \\( k = 20 - n \\).\n        - The number of pairs when \\( k = 11 \\) equals the number of pairs when \\( k = 9 \\):\n          - \\( k = 19 \\) (same as \\( k = 1 \\))\n          - \\( k = 18 \\) (same as \\( k = 2 \\))\n          - \\( k = 17 \\) (same as \\( k = 3 \\))\n          - ...\n          - \\( k = 12 \\) (same as \\( k = 8 \\)).\n\n10. **Summarize and count the pairs:**\n\n    - From the table we conclude:\n\n    Values of \\( k \\)        & Number of values of \\( n \\)\\\\\n    \\hline \n    \\( 20 \\) & \\( 20 \\) \\\\\n    \\( 10 \\) & \\( 10 \\) \\\\\n    \\( 5, 15 \\) & \\( 5 \\) \\\\\n    \\( 4, 8, 12, 16 \\) & \\( 4 \\) \\\\\n    \\( 2, 6, 14, 18 \\) & \\( 2 \\) \\\\\\\n    \\( 1, 3, 7, 9, 11, 13, 17, 19 \\) & \\( 1 \\) \\\\\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total pairs:} = 20 + 10 + 2 \\times 5 + 4 \\times 4 + 4 \\times 2 + 8 \\times 1 = 72.\n\\]\n\nConclusion: \\(\\boxed{72}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given the ellipse \\(\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 \\ (a>b>0)\\), which always passes through the point \\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\), and the length of its major axis ranges from \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}\\), determine the range of the eccentricity of the ellipse.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem step by step. So, we have an ellipse given by the equation \\(\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1\\) where \\(a > b > 0\\). It always passes through the point \\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\). The length of its major axis is between \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) and \\(\\sqrt{6}\\). We need to find the range of the eccentricity of this ellipse.\n\nFirst, let's recall some basic facts about ellipses. The standard form is \\(\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1\\), where \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis and \\(b\\) is the semi-minor axis. The length of the major axis is \\(2a\\), so if the major axis length ranges from \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}\\), then the semi-major axis \\(a\\) should range from \\(\\sqrt{5}/2\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}/2\\). Wait, hold on, let me check that.\n\nActually, the major axis length is \\(2a\\). The problem states that this length ranges from \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}\\). Therefore, \\(2a\\) is in \\([\\sqrt{5}, \\sqrt{6}]\\), which means \\(a\\) is in \\([\\sqrt{5}/2, \\sqrt{6}/2]\\). Let me confirm:\n\nIf major axis length is from \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}\\), then since major axis is \\(2a\\), dividing both ends by 2 gives \\(a\\) between \\(\\sqrt{5}/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt{6}/2\\). Yes, that's correct. So, \\(a \\in [\\sqrt{5}/2, \\sqrt{6}/2]\\).\n\nNow, the ellipse passes through the point \\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\). Plugging this point into the ellipse equation should satisfy it. Let's do that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating the numerators:\n\n\\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\\). So the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1/2}{a^2} + \\frac{1/2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 to simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2} = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, that's an equation relating \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Now, our goal is to find the range of the eccentricity \\(e\\), which is given by \\(e = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{b^2}{a^2}}\\). So, if we can find the possible values of \\(b^2/a^2\\), then we can find \\(e\\).\n\nBut first, let's express \\(b^2\\) in terms of \\(a^2\\) using the equation we derived. From:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2} = 2\n\\]\n\nLet's solve for \\(1/b^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{b^2} = 2 - \\frac{1}{a^2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nb^2 = \\frac{1}{2 - \\frac{1}{a^2}} = \\frac{a^2}{2a^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(b^2 = \\frac{a^2}{2a^2 - 1}\\). Since \\(a > b > 0\\), this must hold true. Let's check if this expression for \\(b^2\\) makes sense. The denominator \\(2a^2 - 1\\) must be positive because \\(b^2\\) is positive. So,\n\n\\(2a^2 - 1 > 0 \\implies a^2 > \\frac{1}{2} \\implies a > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). But our given range for \\(a\\) is from \\(\\sqrt{5}/2 \\approx 1.118\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}/2 \\approx 1.2247\\). Since \\(\\sqrt{5}/2 \\approx 1.118\\) is greater than \\(\\sqrt{2}/2 \\approx 0.707\\), the denominator is positive in the given range, so that's okay.\n\nNow, let's substitute \\(b^2\\) into the eccentricity formula:\n\n\\[\ne = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{b^2}{a^2}} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{\\frac{a^2}{2a^2 - 1}}{a^2}} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{2a^2 - 1}}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{1}{2a^2 - 1} = \\frac{(2a^2 - 1) - 1}{2a^2 - 1} = \\frac{2a^2 - 2}{2a^2 - 1} = \\frac{2(a^2 - 1)}{2a^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\nSo, the eccentricity becomes:\n\n\\[\ne = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(a^2 - 1)}{2a^2 - 1}}\n\\]\n\nNow, our task is to find the range of \\(e\\) as \\(a\\) varies between \\(\\sqrt{5}/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt{6}/2\\). To do this, we can analyze how the expression inside the square root varies with \\(a\\) in the given interval.\n\nLet me first compute \\(a^2\\) for the endpoints:\n\nFor \\(a = \\sqrt{5}/2\\), \\(a^2 = 5/4 = 1.25\\)\n\nFor \\(a = \\sqrt{6}/2\\), \\(a^2 = 6/4 = 3/2 = 1.5\\)\n\nSo, \\(a^2\\) ranges from 1.25 to 1.5.\n\nLet\u2019s substitute these into the expression for \\(e\\):\n\nFirst, at \\(a^2 = 1.25\\):\n\nNumerator inside the square root: \\(2(1.25 - 1) = 2(0.25) = 0.5\\)\n\nDenominator: \\(2(1.25) - 1 = 2.5 - 1 = 1.5\\)\n\nSo, \\(e = \\sqrt{0.5 / 1.5} = \\sqrt{1/3} \\approx 0.577\\)\n\nAt \\(a^2 = 1.5\\):\n\nNumerator: \\(2(1.5 - 1) = 2(0.5) = 1\\)\n\nDenominator: \\(2(1.5) - 1 = 3 - 1 = 2\\)\n\nSo, \\(e = \\sqrt{1 / 2} = \\sqrt{0.5} \\approx 0.707\\)\n\nTherefore, as \\(a^2\\) increases from 1.25 to 1.5, \\(e\\) increases from \\(\\sqrt{1/3}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{1/2}\\). But we need to confirm whether the expression for \\(e\\) is increasing or decreasing over this interval. Let's check the derivative to see the trend.\n\nLet\u2019s consider \\(e(a)\\) as a function of \\(a^2\\), but perhaps it's easier to let \\(t = a^2\\), so \\(t \\in [1.25, 1.5]\\). Then,\n\n\\(e(t) = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(t - 1)}{2t - 1}}\\)\n\nWe can compute the derivative of \\(e(t)\\) with respect to \\(t\\) to determine if it's increasing or decreasing.\n\nFirst, let\u2019s write \\(e(t)\\) as:\n\n\\(e(t) = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(t - 1)}{2t - 1}} = \\left(\\frac{2(t - 1)}{2t - 1}\\right)^{1/2}\\)\n\nLet\u2019s compute the derivative \\(e'(t)\\):\n\nLet \\(f(t) = \\frac{2(t - 1)}{2t - 1}\\), so \\(e(t) = f(t)^{1/2}\\). Then,\n\n\\(e'(t) = \\frac{1}{2} f(t)^{-1/2} \\cdot f'(t)\\)\n\nFirst, compute \\(f'(t)\\):\n\n\\(f(t) = \\frac{2(t - 1)}{2t - 1}\\)\n\nUsing the quotient rule:\n\n\\(f'(t) = \\frac{2(2t - 1) - 2(t - 1)(2)}{(2t - 1)^2}\\)\n\nWait, let me compute numerator step by step:\n\nNumerator derivative: 2*(2t - 1) - 2(t - 1)*2\n\nWait, no. Wait, quotient rule is [num\u2019 * den - num * den\u2019] / den\u00b2\n\nWait, let me rephrase:\n\nIf \\(f(t) = \\frac{N(t)}{D(t)}\\), then \\(f'(t) = \\frac{N'(t) D(t) - N(t) D'(t)}{D(t)^2}\\).\n\nSo, \\(N(t) = 2(t - 1)\\), so \\(N'(t) = 2(1) = 2\\)\n\n\\(D(t) = 2t - 1\\), so \\(D'(t) = 2\\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\(f'(t) = \\frac{2(2t - 1) - 2(t - 1)(2)}{(2t - 1)^2}\\)\n\nCompute numerator:\n\nFirst term: 2*(2t -1) = 4t -2\n\nSecond term: -2(t -1)*2 = -4(t -1) = -4t +4\n\nTotal numerator: (4t - 2) + (-4t +4) = (4t -4t) + (-2 +4) = 0 + 2 = 2\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\(f'(t) = \\frac{2}{(2t -1)^2}\\)\n\nTherefore, the derivative of \\(f(t)\\) is positive for all \\(t \\neq 1/2\\), which is true in our interval (since \\(t \\geq 1.25 > 1/2\\)). Therefore, \\(f(t)\\) is increasing over the interval \\(t \\in [1.25, 1.5]\\), so \\(e(t) = \\sqrt{f(t)}\\) is also increasing, since the square root of an increasing function is increasing if the function is positive, which it is here.\n\nTherefore, since \\(f(t)\\) is increasing, \\(e(t)\\) is increasing. Thus, when \\(t\\) increases from 1.25 to 1.5, \\(e(t)\\) increases from \\(\\sqrt{1/3}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{1/2}\\). Therefore, the range of the eccentricity is \\([ \\sqrt{1/3}, \\sqrt{1/2} ]\\).\n\nBut let's write these in simplified radical form.\n\n\\(\\sqrt{1/3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), and \\(\\sqrt{1/2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n\nSo, the eccentricity \\(e\\) ranges from \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n\nBut let me verify this conclusion once again to be sure. Since we derived the expression for \\(e\\) in terms of \\(a^2\\), and we found that \\(e\\) is an increasing function of \\(a^2\\) in the given interval, then the minimum \\(e\\) occurs at the minimum \\(a\\) (i.e., \\(a = \\sqrt{5}/2\\)) and the maximum \\(e\\) occurs at the maximum \\(a\\) (i.e., \\(a = \\sqrt{6}/2\\)).\n\nDouble-checking the calculations at the endpoints:\n\nFor \\(a = \\sqrt{5}/2\\):\n\n\\(a^2 = 5/4 = 1.25\\)\n\nCompute \\(e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(1.25 -1)}{2*1.25 -1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2*0.25}{2.5 -1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.5}{1.5}} = \\sqrt{1/3} \u2248 0.577\\), which is \\(\\sqrt{3}/3 \u2248 0.577\\).\n\nFor \\(a = \\sqrt{6}/2\\):\n\n\\(a^2 = 6/4 = 1.5\\)\n\nCompute \\(e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(1.5 -1)}{2*1.5 -1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2*0.5}{3 -1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\sqrt{2}/2 \u2248 0.707\\).\n\nYes, that checks out. Therefore, the range of the eccentricity is from \\(\\sqrt{3}/3\\) to \\(\\sqrt{2}/2\\). So, the answer is \\(\\left[ \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right]\\).\n\nBut just to ensure we haven't missed any constraints, let's verify that with the given point and the major axis length constraints, there aren't any other restrictions on \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). For instance, since \\(a > b\\), we need to make sure that \\(b^2 < a^2\\).\n\nGiven that \\(b^2 = \\frac{a^2}{2a^2 -1}\\), so \\(b^2 = \\frac{a^2}{2a^2 -1}\\). Let's check if \\(b^2 < a^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a^2}{2a^2 -1} < a^2 \\implies \\frac{1}{2a^2 -1} < 1 \\implies 1 < 2a^2 -1 \\implies 2a^2 -1 >1 \\implies 2a^2 >2 \\implies a^2 >1\n\\]\n\nBut in our case, \\(a^2\\) ranges from 1.25 to 1.5, which is greater than 1. Therefore, \\(2a^2 -1 >1\\), so the inequality \\(b^2 < a^2\\) holds true. Therefore, the condition \\(a > b\\) is satisfied throughout the interval. Therefore, there are no additional constraints.\n\nHence, the range of the eccentricity is indeed from \\(\\sqrt{3}/3\\) to \\(\\sqrt{2}/2\\).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe range of the eccentricity of the ellipse is \\boxed{\\left[ \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right]}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given the ellipse \\(\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 \\ (a > b > 0)\\) that passes through the point \\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\) and has a major axis length ranging from \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}\\), we need to determine the range of its eccentricity.\n\n1. **Major Axis Length**:\n   - The length of the major axis is \\(2a\\), which ranges from \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{6}\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(a\\) ranges from \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\).\n\n2. **Ellipse Equation through the Given Point**:\n   - Substituting \\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\) into the ellipse equation:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2}{b^2} = 1\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1/2}{a^2} + \\frac{1/2}{b^2} = 1 \\implies \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2} = 2\n     \\]\n\n3. **Relating \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)**:\n   - Solving for \\(b^2\\):\n     \\[\n     b^2 = \\frac{a^2}{2a^2 - 1}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Eccentricity Calculation**:\n   - Eccentricity \\(e\\) is given by:\n     \\[\n     e = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{b^2}{a^2}} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{2a^2 - 1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(a^2 - 1)}{2a^2 - 1}}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Range of \\(a^2\\)**:\n   - For \\(a = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\), \\(a^2 = 1.25\\).\n   - For \\(a = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\), \\(a^2 = 1.5\\).\n\n6. **Evaluating Eccentricity at Endpoints**:\n   - At \\(a^2 = 1.25\\):\n     \\[\n     e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(1.25 - 1)}{2 \\cdot 1.25 - 1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.5}{1.5}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n     \\]\n   - At \\(a^2 = 1.5\\):\n     \\[\n     e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(1.5 - 1)}{2 \\cdot 1.5 - 1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion**:\n   - The eccentricity \\(e\\) is an increasing function of \\(a^2\\) in the interval \\([1.25, 1.5]\\).\n   - Therefore, the range of the eccentricity is \\(\\left[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right]\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left[ \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right]}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Given the ellipse equation: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1 \\quad \\text{with} \\quad a > b > 0\n   \\]\n\n2. We know a fixed point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$ lies on the ellipse. Substituting this point into the ellipse equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2}{b^2} = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\frac{2}{4}}{a^2} + \\frac{\\frac{2}{4}}{b^2} = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2a^2} + \\frac{1}{2b^2} = 1 \n   \\]\n   Multiplying the entire equation by 2 to clear the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2} = 2 \n   \\]\n   Let this be equation *(1)*.\n\n3. We are given that the range of values for the length of the major axis is $[\\sqrt{5}, \\sqrt{6}]$. The length of the major axis for an ellipse is $2a$.\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{5} \\leq 2a \\leq \\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\leq a \\leq \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Squaring both bounds to eliminate the square root:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2 \\leq a^2 \\leq \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{4} \\leq a^2 \\leq \\frac{6}{4}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{4} \\leq a^2 \\leq \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   \n4. From equation *(1)*, rearrange to solve for $b^2$:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{b^2} = 2 - \\frac{1}{a^2}\n   \\]\n   Define the eccentricity $e$ of the ellipse, which is given by:\n   \\[\n   e = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{b^2}{a^2}}\n   \\]\n   Plugging in $b^2$:\n   \\[\n   e = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{a^2(2 - \\frac{1}{a^2})}{a^2}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   e = \\sqrt{1 - (2a^2 - 1)} \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - e^2}{1 - e^2}}\n   \\]\n\n5. Combinin