\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the maximum value of the function \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 5} + \\sqrt{24 - 3x} \\), we need to analyze its behavior within its domain and determine the critical points.\n\n1. **Determine the domain of \\( f(x) \\):**\n   The function \\( f(x) \\) is defined for the values of \\( x \\) where both \\( \\sqrt{x - 5} \\) and \\( \\sqrt{24 - 3x} \\) are real and non-negative.\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x - 5 \\geq 0 \\\\\n   24 - 3x \\geq 0\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving these inequalities:\n   \\[\n   x \\geq 5\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   3x \\leq 24 \\implies x \\leq 8\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the domain of \\( f(x) \\) is \\( 5 \\leq x \\leq 8 \\).\n\n2. **Find the critical points:**\n   To find the critical points, we calculate the derivative \\( f'(x) \\) and solve \\( f'(x) = 0 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 5} + \\sqrt{24 - 3x}\n   \\]\n\n   Differentiating \\( f(x) \\) with respect to \\( x \\):\n\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\sqrt{x - 5} \\right) + \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\sqrt{24 - 3x} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Using the chain rule:\n\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x-5}} - \\frac{3}{2 \\sqrt{24-3x}}\n   \\]\n\n   Setting the derivative equal to zero:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x-5}} - \\frac{3}{2 \\sqrt{24-3x}} = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{24-3x}}\n   \\]\n\n   Squaring both sides:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{24-3x}} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{x-5} = \\frac{9}{24-3x}\n   \\]\n\n   Cross-multiplying:\n\n   \\[\n   24 - 3x = 9(x - 5)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   24 - 3x = 9x - 45\n   \\]\n\n   Combining like terms:\n\n   \\[\n   24 + 45 = 12x\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   69 = 12x\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{69}{12} = \\frac{23}{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluate the function at the endpoints and the critical point:**\n\n   At \\( x = 5 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   f(5) = \\sqrt{