et's show that P^{[k+1]}(x) + P^{[k+1]}(1 -x)=1.\n\nCompute P^{[k+1]}(x)=P^{[k]}(P(x)).\n\nSimilarly, P^{[k+1]}(1 -x)=P^{[k]}(P(1 -x)).\n\nBut P(1 -x)=1 - P(x), from the base case. Therefore,\n\nP^{[k+1]}(1 -x)=P^{[k]}(1 - P(x)).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, P^{[k]}(y) + P^{[k]}(1 - y)=1 for any y. Let y = P(x), then P^{[k]}(P(x)) + P^{[k]}(1 - P(x))=1. But P^{[k]}(P(x))=P^{[k+1]}(x), and P^{[k]}(1 - P(x))=P^{[k+1]}(1 -x). Therefore,\n\nP^{[k+1]}(x) + P^{[k+1]}(1 -x)=1.\n\nThus, by induction, for all n\u22651, P^{[n]}(x) + P^{[n]}(1 -x)=1. Therefore, integrating P^{[n]}(x) over [0,1] gives 1/2 for any n.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1/2.\n\nBut let me verify this with n=2. Compute I_2=\u222b\u2080\u00b9 P(P(x)) dx. From the symmetry, it should be 1/2. Let's check with substitution.\n\nLet\u2019s make the substitution u =1 -x. Then when x=0, u=1; x=1, u=0. So:\n\nI_2 = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 P(P(x)) dx = \u222b\u2081\u2070 P(P(1 -u)) (-du) = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 P(P(1 -u)) du.\n\nBut P(P(1 -u)) = P(1 - P(u)), because P(1 -u) =1 - P(u). Therefore,\n\nI_2=\u222b\u2080\u00b9 P(1 - P(u)) du.\n\nBut from the symmetry of P, P(1 - y) =1 - P(y). Therefore, P(1 - P(u))=1 - P(P(u)).\n\nTherefore,\n\nI_2=\u222b\u2080\u00b9 [1 - P(P(u))] du = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 1 du - \u222b\u2080\u00b9 P(P(u)) du =1 - I_2.\n\nTherefore, I_2=1 -I_2 \u21d2 2I_2=1 \u21d2I_2=1/2. This confirms that I_2=1/2. Similarly, by induction, all I_n=1/2. Therefore, regardless of the number of iterations, the integral remains 1/2. So the answer is 1/2.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}", "deepseek_solution": "To evaluate \\(\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx\\) where \\(P(x) = x^3 - \\frac{3}{2}x^2 + x + \\frac{1}{4}\\), we start by analyzing the function \\(P(x)\\) and its iterates.\n\nFirst, we note that \\(P(x)\\) has a fixed point at \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and satisfies the symmetry property \\(P(x) + P(1 - x) = 1\\). This symmetry is crucial because it implies that the integral of \\(P(x)\\) over the interval \\([0, 1]\\) is preserved under composition.\n\nWe verify the symmetry property:\n\\[\nP(1 - x) = (1 - x)^3 - \\frac{3}{2}(1 - x)^2 + (1 - x) + \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\nExpanding and simplifying, we find:\n\\[\nP(x) + P(1 - x) = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we use induction to show that this symmetry property holds for all iterates \\(P^{[n]}(x)\\). The base case \\(n = 1\\) is already verified. Assuming \\(P^{[n]}(x) + P^{[n]}(1 - x) = 1\\) holds for some \\(n\\), we show it holds for \\(n + 1\\):\n\\[\nP^{[n+1]}(x) = P^{[n]}(P(x)) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad P^{[n+1]}(1 - x) = P^{[n]}(P(1 - x)) = P^{[n]}(1 - P(x))\n\\]\nUsing the induction hypothesis, we get:\n\\[\nP^{[n]}(P(x)) + P^{[n]}(1 - P(x)) = 1\n\\]\nThus, \\(P^{[n+1]}(x) + P^{[n+1]}(1 - x) = 1\\).\n\nGiven this symmetry, the integral of \\(P^{[n]}(x)\\) over \\([0, 1]\\) is:\n\\[\n\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[n]}(x) \\, dx + \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[n]}(1 - x) \\, dx = \\int_{0}^{1} 1 \\, dx = 1\n\\]\nSince the integrals are equal, each must be \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\). Therefore, for any \\(n \\geq 1\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[n]}(x) \\, dx = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Given the function \\( P(x) = x^3 - \\frac{3}{2} x^2 + x + \\frac{1}{4} \\).\n\n2. Define \\( P^{[1]}(x) = P(x) \\) and \\( P^{[n+1]}(x) = P^{[n]}(P(x)) \\) for \\( n \\geq 1 \\).\n\n3. We are asked to evaluate \\( \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx \\).\n\n4. First, observe the functional symmetry property of \\( P(x) \\):\n   $$\n   P(1-x) = (1-x)^3 - \\frac{3}{2} (1-x)^2 + (1-x) + \\frac{1}{4}\n   $$\n   Expand and simplify \\( P(1-x) \\):\n   \\begin{align*}\n   (1-x)^3 &= 1 - 3x + 3x^2 - x^3 \\\\\n   -\\frac{3}{2}(1-x)^2 &= -\\frac{3}{2}(1 - 2x + x^2) = -\\frac{3}{2} + 3x - \\frac{3}{2}x^2 \\\\\n   (1-x) &= 1 - x \\\\\n   \\end{align*}\n   Summing these, we get:\n   \\begin{align*}\n   P(1-x) &= 1 - 3x + 3x^2 - x^3 - \\frac{3}{2} + 3x - \\frac{3}{2} x^2 + 1 - x + \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n   &= 1 - x^3 - \\frac{1}{2} x^2 + \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\end{align*}\n\n   Further simplification yields:\n   $$\n   P(1-x) = 1 - P(x)\n   $$\n\n5. Using this property, we proceed to evaluate the integral.\n\n6. By induction, for all positive integers \\( k \\),\n   $$\n   P^{[k]}(1-x) = 1 - P^{[k]}(x)\n   $$\n\n7. We substitute this into the integral:\n   $$\n   \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx = \\int_{0}^{1} 1 - P^{[2004]}(1-x) \\, dx\n   $$\n   By the given symmetry property \\( P^{[2004]}(1-x) = 1 - P^{[2004]}(x) \\), we get:\n   $$\n   \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx = 1 - \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(1-x) \\, dx\n   $$\n   Substituting \\( u = 1 - x \\) with \\( du = -dx \\):\n   $$\n   \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(1-x) \\, dx = \\int_{1}^{0} P^{[2004]}(u) \\, (-du) = \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(u) \\, du\n   $$\n   Therefore,\n   $$\n   \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx = 1 - \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(u) \\, du = 1 - \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx\n   $$\n\n8. Let \\( I = \\int_{0}^{1} P^{[2004]}(x) \\, dx \\).\n\n   Then, we have,\n   $$\n   I = 1 - I\n   $$\n\n9. Adding \\( I \\) to both sides,\n   $$\n   2I = 1\n   $$\n   Dividing both sides by 2,\n   $$\n   I = \\frac{1}{2}\n   $$\n\n### Conclusion:\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n$$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nOn the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{25}+\\frac{y^{2}}{9}=1$, different points $A\\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\\right), B\\left(4, \\frac{9}{5}\\right), C\\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\\right)$ are such that their distances to the focus $F(4,0)$ form an arithmetic sequence. If the perpendicular bisector of segment $A C$ intersects the $x$-axis at point $T$, find the slope $k$ of the line $BT$. $\\qquad$ .", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. First, let's make sure I understand the problem correctly.\n\nWe have an ellipse given by the equation x\u00b2/25 + y\u00b2/9 = 1. So, this is a stand