} = -\\frac{x_1 - x_2}{y_1 - y_2}\\left( x - \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2} \\right)\n   $$\n\n5. By setting \\(y = 0\\) to find intersection point \\(T\\):\n    $$\n    0 - \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} = -\\frac{x_1 - x_2}{y_1 - y_2} \\left( x_T - \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2} \\right)\n    $$\n    Solving for \\(x_T\\):\n    $$\n    x_T = \\frac{y_1^2 - y_2^2}{2(x_1 - x_2)} + \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}\n    $$\n\n6. Given \\(A, C\\) are on ellipse:\n    $$\n    \\begin{array}{l}\n    9 x_1^2 + 25 y_1^2 = 225 \\\\\n    9 x_2^2 + 25 y_2^2 = 225\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Subtracting, we get:\n    $$\n    25(y_1^2 - y_2^2) = -9(x_1^2 - x_2^2)\n    $$\n    Factoring the right side:\n    $$ \n    -9(x_1^2 - x_2^2) = -9(x_1 + x_2)(x_1 - x_2)\n    $$\n\n7. Hence:\n    $$\n    \\frac{y_1^2 - y_2^2}{x_1 - x_2} = -\\frac{9}{25}(x_1 + x_2)\n    $$\n\n8. Substituting this in \\(x_T\\) gives an expression:\n    $$\n    x_T = -\\frac{9}{25} \\cdot \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2} + \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2} = \\frac{16}{25} \\cdot 8 = \\frac{64}{25}\n    $$\n\n9. Finally, slope \\( k_{BT} \\):\n    $$\n    k_{BT} = \\frac{\\frac{9}{5}}{4 - \\frac{64}{25}} = \\frac{\\frac{9}{5}}{\\frac{100 - 64}{25}} = \\frac{\\frac{9}{5}}{\\frac{36}{25}} = \\frac{9}{5} \\cdot \\frac{25}{36} = \\frac{45}{36} = \\frac{5}{4}\n    $$\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{4}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "They drew an acute-angled $\\triangle ABC$ on the board and marked the bases of the altitudes as $A_1$, $B_1$, and $C_1$. Then, the entire drawing was erased except for the points $A_1$, $B_1$, and $C_1$. Is it possible to reconstruct the original $\\triangle ABC$ using a compass and straightedge?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the problem is about whether we can reconstruct the original triangle ABC given just the bases of its altitudes, A1, B1, and C1, using only a compass and straightedge. Hmm. Let me think through this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's recall s