 four differences: x, y, x + y, x - y. So y - x is not one of them unless y - x equals one of the four. For example, if x - y is one of the four, then y - x is its negative. But the problem allows the difference to be x - y, so if two numbers differ by y - x, which is -(x - y), that difference is not listed unless it's explicitly included. However, the problem statement includes x - y as one of the allowable differences, so technically, the difference must be exactly x - y, not y - x. But in integers, the difference a - b can be positive or negative. So, if the problem is considering unordered pairs, then the absolute difference would matter, but the problem says \"difference has one of the following values\", which might imply the actual difference, not absolute.\n\nThis is a critical point. If the problem requires the difference to be exactly x, y, x + y, or x - y (considering sign), then in the set {n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y}, the differences y - x might not be in the target set unless it's equal to one of the four. For example, if x = 1 and y = 3, then y - x = 2, which is x - y = -2, so if the problem allows negative differences, then -2 is allowed as x - y. So if two numbers differ by 2, which is y - x, then the difference is 2, which is not in {1, 3, 4, -2}. Wait, but x - y = -2 is in the list. So if the difference is 2, it's not in the list. But if two numbers differ by -2 (i.e., a - b = -2), then that is in the list as x - y. But the problem statement says \"difference has one of the following values: x, y, x+y, x-y\". So if the difference is -2, which is x - y (when x=1, y=3), then that's acceptable. So in the example, if two numbers differ by 2, which is y - x, then it's not in the list, but if they differ by -2, which is x - y, then it is. But difference is directional. So if a - b = -2, then b - a = 2. So unless the problem considers unordered pairs, where the difference is just the magnitude, this could be an issue.\n\nThe problem statement says: \"two integers of the same color whose difference has one of the following values: x, y, x+y, x-y\". The difference between two integers a and b is typically defined as a - b, which can be positive or negative. Therefore, the problem is asking for a - b \u2208 {x, y, x + y, x - y}. So, for example, if a - b = x - y, which could be negative if x < y, then that's acceptable.\n\nTherefore, in the set {n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y}, the differences between any two numbers are:\n\n- x, y, x + y, y - x, x - y, etc.\n\nWait, let's list all differences:\n\n1. (n + x) - n = x\n2. (n + y) - n = y\n3. (n + x + y) - n = x + y\n4. (n + x) - (n + y) = x - y\n5. (n + x + y) - (n + x) = y\n6. (n + x + y) - (n + y) = x\n\nSo the differences are x, y, x + y, x - y. The other differences are repeats: y - x is -(x - y), but we already have x - y in the list. Similarly, others are duplicates. So in this set, the possible differences are exactly x, y, x + y, x - y. Therefore, if two numbers in this set share the same color, their difference must be one of these four values. And since there are four numbers and four colors, it's possible that each number is a different color, avoiding a monochromatic pair. However, since this is true for every integer n, then for each n, the four numbers n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y must all be different colors. But since the integers are infinite, this would require an infinite number of colors, which we don't have. Wait, but we only have four colors. So if for each n, the four numbers are colored with four different colors, then when we consider n + x, the same applies: n + x, n + x + x, n + x + y, etc., must also be colored with four different colors. This creates a repeating pattern, but with four colors, the pattern would have to repeat every four steps, but with the addition of x and y, which are arbitrary odd integers, this might not hold.\n\nBut actually, the key insight is that if every quadruple {n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y} is colored with all four colors, then the coloring must be periodic with some period related to x and y. However, since x and y are arbitrary odd integers with |x| \u2260 |y|, such a period would have to accommodate all possible combinations, which isn't feasible with a four-color palette. Therefore, by the pigeonhole principle applied across the entire integer set, there must exist some n where two numbers in the quadruple {n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y} share the same color, hence providing the required monochromatic pair with one of the desired differences.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion follows from applying the pigeonhole principle to each such quadruple and recognizing that a four-coloring cannot consistently avoid monochromatic pairs across all quadruples.", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the problem, we employ the pigeonhole principle and analyze specific quadruples of integers. Here's the step-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Consider Quadruples**: For any integer \\( n \\), examine the set \\( \\{n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y\\} \\). These four numbers must be colored with the four available colors.\n\n2. **Possible Differences**: The differences between any two numbers in this set are:\n   - \\( (n + x) - n = x \\)\n   - \\( (n + y) - n = y \\)\n   - \\( (n + x + y) - n = x + y \\)\n   - \\( (n + x) - (n + y) = x - y \\)\n   - \\( (n + x + y) - (n + x) = y \\)\n   - \\( (n + x + y) - (n + y) = x \\)\n   \n   The relevant differences here are \\( x, y, x + y, \\) and \\( x - y \\).\n\n3. **Pigeonhole Principle**: Since there are four numbers in the quadruple and four colors, if each number were colored uniquely, no monochromatic pair would exist. However, as we consider all integers \\( n \\), maintaining unique coloring for every such quadruple is impossible with only four colors. This is because overlapping quadruples (e.g., \\( n + x \\) becomes part of the next quadruple) force color repetitions.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: By the pigeonhole principle, there must exist some integer \\( n \\) where two numbers in \\( \\{n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y\\} \\) share the same color. Their difference will be one of \\( x, y, x + y, \\) or \\( x - y \\), thereby satisfying the problem's condition.\n\n**Final Answer**: In any 4-coloring of the integers, there exist two integers of the same color differing by \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( x + y \\), or \\( x - y \\). This follows from the pigeonhole principle applied to quadruples \\( \\{n, n + x, n + y, n + x + y\\} \\), ensuring a monochromatic pair with one of the required differences. \\(\\boxed{\\text{Such a monochromatic pair necessarily exists}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define the coloring function**:\n   Let \\( f: \\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrow \\{ R, G, B, Y \\} \\) be a function that assigns a color, either Red, Green, Blue, or Yellow, to each integer. \n\n2. **Hypothesis**:\n   Suppose that for every \\( a \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\), the set \\( \\{ f(a), f(a + x), f(a + y), f(a + x + y) \\} \\) contains all four colors. That means for any integer \\( a \\), the elements must be colored using all four different colors distinctly.\n\n3. **Mapping to integer lattice**:\n   Define a new coloring of the integer lattice \\( g: \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrow \\{ R, G, B, Y \\} \\) by the rule:\n   \\[\n   g(i, j) = f(xi + yj)\n   \\]\n   Here, the value \\( xi + yj \\) is colored according to the original coloring function \\( f \\).\n\n4. **Property of lattice coloring**:\n   According to our hypothesis, every unit square in \\( g \\) must have vertices colored by four different colors. That is, for any integers \\( i \\) and \\( j \\), we would have:\n   \\[\n   \\{ g(i, j), g(i+1, j), g(i, j+1), g(i+1, j+1) \\} = \\{ R, G, B, Y \\}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Periodicity in rows/columns**:\n   If there is a row or column with a period of 2, then applying the condition to adjacent unit squares, it must follow that all rows or all columns have a period of 2. \n\n6. **Contradiction in periodicity**:\n   Consider an example where we have a sequence of three distinct colors in a row, say it is GRY. Following the coloring rule, the next row must then contain YBG in those columns. Over subsequent rows, the columns must alternate colors:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{Row 1:} & G & R & Y \\\\\n   &\\text{Row 2:} & Y & B & G \\\\\n   &\\text{Row 3:} & G & R & Y \\\\\n   &\\text{Row 4:} & Y & B & G \\\\\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   This leads to rows having a period of 2 while each column alternates distinctly.\n\n7. **General case for coloring period**:\n   Either all rows or all columns must have a period of 2. Without loss of generality, assume all rows have a period of 2. That is, we assume:\n   \\[\n   g(i, j) = g(i + 2, j)\n   \\]\n\n8. **Contradiction involving odd \\( x \\)**:\n   Assuming \\( g(0, 0) \\) and \\( g(1, 0) \\) are \\( G \\) and \\( B \\), respectively, implies:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   g(0, 0) &= G, \\quad \\text{(even rows have } \\{ G, B \\}) \\\\\n   g(1, 0) &= B \\quad \\text{(odd rows have } \\{ R, Y \\})\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x \\) is odd, it will make \\( g(y, 0) \\) and \\( g(0, x) \\) correspond to different rows or positions, giving different colors.\n\n9. **Irreconcilable difference**:\n   Because \\( g(y, 0) \\) should equal \\( g(0, x) \\) when following the form \\( g(y, 0) = f(xy) = g(0, x) \\), and \\( f(xy) \\) contradicts this as these must be the same color, contradicting our assumption.\n\n10. **Conclusion**:\n    Thus our assumption that all four positions \\( \\{ a, a + x, a + y, a + x + y \\} \\) are colored differently cannot hold. Hence, there must be at least two integers of the same color whose difference is one of \\( x, y, x + y, x - y \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Therefore, the statement of the problem is proven to be true.}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If \\(\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{x}} = 2\\), then the value of \\(x\\) is\n(A) 8