e entire right-hand side is:\n        $$\n        2r - (n + r + r) = 2r - 1n - 2r = -n.\n        $$\n\n3. **Equivalence of both sides**:\n    - For any arbitrary prime \\( p \\),\n      - The exponent \\( p \\) in the left-hand side is \\(-n\\)\n      - The exponent \\( p \\) in the right-hand side is \\(-n\\)\n    - Therefore, both sides of the equation have the same factors of each prime.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\nSince the expressions are equivalent for any chosen prime \\( p \\), the original identity holds true:\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{[a, b, c]^2}{[a, b] \\cdot [b, c] \\cdot [c, a]} = \\frac{(a, b, c)^2}{(a, b) \\cdot (b, c) \\cdot (c, a)}}.\n$$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given \\( f(x) = \\frac{\\ln \\left(x^{2}+1\\right)}{(x+1)+3} \\), find the value of \\( x \\) such that the functions \\( y = f(3-x) \\) and \\( y = f(3+x) \\) are symmetric about a line.\n\nA. \\( x = -3 \\)  \nB. \\( x = 0 \\)  \nC. \\( x = 3 \\)  \nD. \\( x = 6 \\)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the value of x where the functions y = f(3 - x) and y = f(3 + x) are symmetric about a line. The options are A. x = -3, B. x = 0, C. x = 3, D. x = 6. Hmm, let's start by understanding what symmetry about a line means here. Usually, symmetry about a line could mean reflection symmetry. For example, if two functions are symmetric about the y-axis, then one is the mirror image of the other across the y-axis. Similarly, symmetry about another vertical line, say x = a, would mean that for each point (a + h, k) on one function, there's a corresponding point (a - h, k) on the other function.\n\nGiven that the two functions here are y = f(3 - x) and y = f(3 + x), maybe their symmetry is about the y-axis or some other vertical line. Wait, let me check. If we consider the variable x, then for f(3 - x), replacing x with -x would give f(3 + x), which is the other function. So, does that mean they are symmetric about the y-axi