athbf{N}\\} \\), choose \\( x_i \\) such that differences \\( x_j - x_i \\) avoid \\( 2^m - 2^n \\). By induction, such \\( x_i \\) exist, making \\( A \\) a **k-set for any \\( k \\)**.\n   - *Co-finite Sets*: If \\( A = \\mathbf{Z} \\setminus \\{0\\} \\), no two translates \\( x_i + A \\) are disjoint because their intersection is co-finite. **Co-finite sets are not k-sets for \\( k \\geq 2 \\)**.\n\n---\n\n### **General Properties**\n- **Density Argument**: If \\( A \\) has asymptotic density \\( \\alpha \\), then \\( k\\alpha \\leq 1 \\). For example, \\( A = d\\mathbf{Z} \\) has density \\( 1/d \\), achieving equality.\n- **Avoiding Differences**: For \\( A \\) to be a k-set, the differences \\( \\{a - b : a, b \\in A\\} \\) must not align with \\( x_j - x_i \\). This is always possible for finite \\( A \\) or sets with sparse differences.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**\n- **Finite Sets**: Always k-sets for any \\( k \\).\n- **Periodic Sets**: Maximum \\( k \\) equals the period \\( d \\).\n- **Infinite Sets**: Depends on structure; sparse sets can be k-sets for any \\( k \\), while dense/co-finite sets cannot.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nA set \\( A \\subseteq \\mathbf{Z} \\) is a k-set if it allows \\( k \\) disjoint translates. Finite sets and strategically structured infinite sets (e.g., sparsely spaced) satisfy this for any \\( k \\), while periodic sets are limited by their period.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven:\n1. \\( A \\subseteq \\mathbf{Z} \\).\n2. There exist \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_k \\in \\mathbf{Z} \\) such that for all \\( i, j \\) where \\( 1 \\leq i < j \\leq k \\),\n   \\[\n   (x_i + A) \\cap (x_j + A) = \\varnothing.\n   \\]\nHere, \\( x + A = \\{x + a \\mid a \\in A\\} \\).\n\nWe need to prove the following:\nIf sets \\( A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_t \\) are \\( k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_t \\)-sets that cover \\( \\mathbf{Z} \\), i.e.,\n\\[\nA_1 \\cup A_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup A_t = \\mathbf{Z},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{k_1} + \\frac{1}{k_2} + \\cdots