\n2. Similarly, P = v: adjacent to u (since uv is an edge), so not counted.\n\n3. For P \u2260 u, v: P is not adjacent to u and not adjacent to v. So, the number here is (n - 2) - [number of neighbors of u + number of neighbors of v - number of common neighbors of u and v].\n\nTherefore, the total number of P (including u and v) not adjacent to u or v is equal to 0 (from P = u and P = v) plus [(n - 2) - (deg(u) + deg(v) - |N(u) \u2229 N(v)|)]. Therefore, the total is (n - 2) - deg(u) - deg(v) + |N(u) \u2229 N(v)|.\n\nBut since uv is an edge, and the graph is triangle-free, u and v cannot have any common neighbors. Because if there is a common neighbor w, then u, v, w would form a triangle. Therefore, in a triangle-free graph, for any edge uv, |N(u) \u2229 N(v)| = 0. So, in this case, the number of vertices P not adjacent to u or v is (n - 2) - deg(u) - deg(v).\n\nTherefore, for each edge uv, the number of P such that P is not adjacent to u or v is (n - 2 - deg(u) - deg(v)).\n\nBut wait, is that right? Let me check.\n\nSuppose we have edge uv. Then, the number of vertices P \u2260 u, v that are not adjacent to u or v is (n - 2) - [deg(u) + deg(v) - |N(u) \u2229 N(v)|]. But since the graph is triangle-free, |N(u) \u2229 N(v)| = 0. Therefore, that becomes (n - 2) - deg(u) - deg(v). Then, adding back in the cases where P = u or P = v. However, as we saw before, P = u is adjacent to v (since uv is an edge), so P = u is adjacent to v, hence not counted. Similarly for P = v. Therefore, the total number of P (including u and v) not adjacent to u or v is (n - 2) - deg(u) - deg(v). So, each edge uv contributes (n - 2 - deg(u) - deg(v)) to the sum over P of e(N(P)^c). Therefore, sum_{P} e(N(P)^c) = sum_{uv \u2208 E} (n - 2 - deg(u) - deg(v)).\n\nTherefore, the average E = (1/n) * sum_{uv \u2208 E} (n - 2 - deg(u) - deg(v)).\n\nLet me simplify that expression. So, E = (1/n) * [k(n - 2) - sum_{uv \u2208 E} (deg(u) + deg(v))].\n\nBut note that sum_{uv \u2208 E} (deg(u) + deg(v)) 